WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.234
-You look gorgeous.
It's always great

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.501
to see you, bud.
-So nice to see you.

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:05.167
It hasn't been that long.
-It hasn't been that long.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.133
We saw each other
at a big night out,

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:09.634
opening night, Broadway,
"Sweeney Todd."

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:12.901
We saw each other.
I sat kind of across from you.

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:14.534
-I'd like to thank you
for your laughter.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:16.901
-Thank you.
-You were really vocal and I --

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.267
-Was I laughing too hard?

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.167
-No, there's no such thing
as laughing too hard in a --

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.567
-You could hear me laughing?

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:22.801
-Oh, yeah,
we identified your laugh.

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:25.033
[ Laughter ]
But it's a comedy.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.567
-Yeah, it is.
-Yeah. And, when you laugh,

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:28.300
you encourage other people
around you to laugh

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.567
because, if you think it's
funny, then it must be funny,

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.334
so, I just want
to say thank you.

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:34.534
-But now I'm a little
self-conscious because was I --

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.000
-No, keep giving it.
It's really nice.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.200
-Now I'm thinking I don't know
what laugh I gave.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.601
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:44.534
Your husband, Thomas Kail,
amazing director, amazing guy.

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:46.133
What a show, by the way,
"Sweeney Todd."

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.667
-Yeah, it's really magnificent.
-Everybody -- Josh Groban,

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.033
Annaleigh Ashford.
Unbelievable.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:52.334
Gaten Matarazzo

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:53.834
[ Cheering ]
from "Stranger" -- Yeah.

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:56.167
[ Applause ]
You got to see this play.
-It's amazing, yeah.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.400
-Do you get nervous
if it's your husband's play?

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.267
I mean, he's directing.
-I was more nervous for him

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.400
than I ever have been for myself
at any opening

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.934
-Really? You're like...
-that I've ever --
No, absolutely.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:05.200
-..."You guys better laugh
at this scene. It's funny."

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.834
-No, That's why, when I knew
it was you laughing,

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:08.100
I was like, "I love him.
-Yeah!

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.200
-I love him." [ Laughs ]
-Yeah, support him.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.334
Yeah, because
I could imagine that.

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.567
But then he got up at the end

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.267
and also had to do a big speech

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.067
and I was like, "Oh, now."
You think you work hard enough.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:17.434
-Even more nervous.
-Even more nervous.

00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:18.701
But he was great.
He was funny.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.801
He's very intelligent.
I was like -- But I was --

00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:22.000
I looked over at you
for a little bit

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.667
because I wanted
to see how you were.

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:24.834
You were watching him.
You were like,

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:26.067
"Yep, that's right, say that.
Alright."

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.467
[ Laughter ]
"Just like we rehearsed."

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:29.601
-"Good job, honey." Exactly.
"Just like we rehearsed at home.

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:31.734
Good job, honey.
Take it home."

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:34.801
But are you used to Broadway,
now that you got --

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:37.000
you were Tony-nominated?
Did you --

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.167
You did -- Was it "Cabaret"?
I think I saw you.

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.868
-"Cabaret"
and I did "Blackbird"...

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:43.300
-"Blackbird" -- phenomenal.
-...and, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.901
-Did you grow up being
a Broadway kid

00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:46.434
or did you want to do theater?
-You know, I started --

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:48.133
the first thing that I ever did
was theater,

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.634
so it really is
kind of my first love.

00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:51.534
And even singing and dancing,
it was the first thing I did.

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:53.234
I was like a chorus kid
in, you know,

00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:55.567
every production of every
children's theater thing.

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:58.133
-No way.
-Yeah, it makes me feel like
a kid again when I'm onstage.

00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:00.167
It just makes me feel
there's a kind of like freedom

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:02.434
and expansiveness to it that you
really can't get anywhere else.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:03.834
-No, there's nothing
like it, too.

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:05.968
And also to see a Broadway show,
too, I can't help it.

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:07.033
No matter --
-It's so exciting, isn't it?

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.200
-It's so exciting.
They work so hard.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.367
I could never do anything like
that at all,

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:14.534
what you guys do, what they do.
But at the end, I'm always like,

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:17.033
when the standing ovation,
I get so misty

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:18.767
and so emotional,
like they're my kids.

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.367
[ Laughter ]
-Well, because --
-I'm like, "Yes!

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:22.400
You did a great job up there!"

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.200
Like they didn't care about me.
-It really is so brave, though.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.901
Anything can happen. Like so
many things have to go right

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:29.367
to have a perfect show
and so you think like

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:31.000
you just think
about like the vulnerability

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.234
and the heart
of these performers

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:35.634
and it's really on display
in "Sweeney Todd."

00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.567
It's just like it's so fun.

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:39.901
It's like a rock concert
for grown-ups.

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:41.734
-It is.
-It's moving. It's emotional.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:43.868
And it's just incredible to see
people assembled in this way

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.167
and it's such
a big production, too.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.734
It's a 26-piece orchestra
and this enormous cast

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.367
and, you know,
and people are finally

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:50.968
like brought back together
and the energy in the room

00:02:50.968 --> 00:02:53.267
is just, you know.
-Yeah. It was wild.

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:54.334
It was so much fun.

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:55.234
But I was going to say also,

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:56.667
you can sing and dance.

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:58.934
Was it "Greatest Showman"
that I was watching?

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:00.868
You were unbelievable
in that as well.
-Thanks.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:02.801
-Would you ever do another one
of those?

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:03.968
That's a giant.

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:05.501
It was a massive hit
in my house, at least.

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:06.934
-Oh, in my house, too.
-[Both] Yeah.

00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:08.868
-I think the world loved it,
yeah.

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:10.434
-Most [indistinct].
-Most, yeah, yeah.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:13.200
-Yeah, I would absolutely.
Again, like singing and dancing,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.467
you're just sort of so tapped
into joy

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:18.934
and you can't really --
you can't -- you can't be --

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.534
you can't regret anything while
you're singing and dancing.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.567
You can't really like go
backwards in time, you know?

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:26.100
-No, you're doing it
-Because you're so caught up

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.968
in what you're doing and the
thing is moving so quickly,

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.734
so, you you don't really
have time to be like,

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:31.667
"Was that a good way
to say the line?"

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:33.434
-Yeah.
When you're onstage,

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:34.634
are you thinking
about anything else

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.968
or are you just going,
"I'm doing my thing"?

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:40.901
-It's kind of an amazing --
it's an amazing way to --

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.934
It's an amazing kind
of concentration to find.

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:44.734
Because, you know,
because you can't stop it.

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:46.467
It's unstoppable,
unlike [indistinct].

00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:48.033
"Oh, hang on, hang on.
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:49.734
-"Let me try it again."
-Hang on. Let me try it again."

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:51.667
-"Can we start with the first
note? Thank you," yeah.

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.067
-It's like a train
that you can't get off of

00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:55.000
and so it's really exhilarating
because, you know like,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.167
even if you feel like you
missed a moment

00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:58.434
or you slipped something up,

00:03:58.434 --> 00:03:59.868
you don't have a choice
but to keep going

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:02.634
and so it's actually like
it's very enlivening.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:05.067
-Congrats on your fifth
Oscar nomination, by the way.

00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:06.100
-Thank you.
-Ooh.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.100
[ Cheering and applause ]
[Indistinct]

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.634
-Well done.
-Thank you.

00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:12.367
-Come on. You're doing it.
You're doing it pretty great.

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.667
-Thank you.
-Imagine that you're doing --

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:18.434
This is your fifth Oscar
nomination and you're acting --

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:20.367
Steven Spielberg
is directing you

00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:23.167
and you're playing a character
based on his mom.

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.634
-I know.
-Talk about pressure.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:26.601
[ Laughter ]
I mean, forget about singing
and dancing.

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:29.100
This is like, "Did I do it
right, Steven? Is this what --"

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.334
I mean, it's his mom.
-Well, he can tell you.

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:33.734
-Yeah.
-He's the world's
leading authority, so.

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:35.167
-On his mom, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:38.067
-So, if he's happy,
then you can like -- you can --

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:39.734
-How did that call --
Did you get the call

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:40.901
from Steven Spielberg?
Like, "Hey --"

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:42.067
-I don't know why he wanted
to talk to me.

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.400
I could not have imagined

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.701
that he wanted me
to act in a movie.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:45.968
I thought he wanted to like
know where to eat

00:04:45.968 --> 00:04:47.701
in Brooklyn or something.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:49.234
You can help him with that,
too, yeah.

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:50.901
-It's still like halfway
into the Zoom

00:04:50.901 --> 00:04:52.868
when we got on this thing,
I still just didn't know

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:55.234
what I was doing there
or what he could possibly

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:56.634
have wanted to Zoom with me for

00:04:56.634 --> 00:04:59.200
and then it sort of like
went off like a...

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.701
bing!
in my head and I had to sort of

00:05:02.701 --> 00:05:06.767
just stop and ask him like,
"Just so we're super like --

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:10.133
are you --
you want me to be your mom?

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:11.400
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.367
And you loved her?"

00:05:13.367 --> 00:05:16.234
[ Laughter ]
Yes, okay, great.

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:17.801
-Yeah, "I just want
to make sure we're clear,

00:05:17.801 --> 00:05:19.400
that we're both having
the same conversation," yeah.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.067
"You want me to be your mom?"
-"Because I think you just

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.100
asked me to be your mom

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.400
[ Laughter ]
and that would be just

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.868
too crazy."
-It was phenomenal, the movie,

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:28.701
the whole story,
it was just fascinating.

00:05:28.701 --> 00:05:30.400
Everyone did a great job
with this.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.200
But I wanted to ask you --
if you can even zoom in --

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.000
what's it working like
with that monkey?

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:38.167
I thought that was --
I thought that was --

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.133
I would always want --
I really want to act

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:41.200
with a monkey.
-Have you ever worked
with a monkey?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.601
-I wish. I would love to work

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:44.367
with a monkey.
-You should have her
on the show.

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:46.901
-Yeah, I'll book the monkey
tomorrow, yeah, let's go.

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:48.734
[ Laughter ]
-You should book the monkey
as like a recurring --

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:49.934
-What's the monkey's name?
They always have fun names.

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:51.501
-Crystal.
-Crystal the Monkey.

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:52.601
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-Fantastic.

00:05:52.601 --> 00:05:54.534
And Crystal get --

00:05:54.534 --> 00:05:56.133
Is it he or she?
-She.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:59.167
-She. Does she get her own
dressing room, Crystal?

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:01.267
-She has like her own --

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:02.434
You never even see --
-World.

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:04.434
-Yeah. She has her own world.
You never see --

00:06:04.434 --> 00:06:05.767
-Like does she come
in a different outfit

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:06.901
and then get changed
for the movie?

00:06:06.901 --> 00:06:08.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.467
-Well, she does wear a diaper.

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:10.701
-She does?!
-Yeah.

00:06:10.701 --> 00:06:12.467
Come on.
That's so cute. Come on.

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:14.200
[ Laughter ]
I would love it
if the monkey showed up

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.868
like dressed a little detective
or something.

00:06:15.868 --> 00:06:18.734
[ Laughter ]
-Well, she has an incredible
wardrobe. You should --

00:06:18.734 --> 00:06:19.868
-She does?
-You should check

00:06:19.868 --> 00:06:21.467
her out.
-I do want to have her come on.

00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:23.400
-She has red carpet looks also.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.200
-She has read
red carpet looks, too?

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.501
-Aww!
-Did you see Crystal in outfits?

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.701
-Oh, yeah. She's --
I'm not kidding.

00:06:29.701 --> 00:06:31.601
You really should have
the monkey on the show.

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:32.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:34.100
She can learn anything.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.801
There's like nothing
that she has been --

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:36.934
There's nothing
that she's been asked to do

00:06:36.934 --> 00:06:39.133
that she's been unable to do.

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:41.300
Like for this movie,
she learned how to

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.434
dismantle a chandelier
and throw lightbulbs.

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:44.567
[ Laughter and awws ]

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:46.734
-And that wasn't even
on her résumé.

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:48.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:50.167
-The only thing that's hard
about working with a monkey is

00:06:50.167 --> 00:06:52.400
like you can really
get upstaged.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:53.767
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:56.033
You go, "Oh, please. You're
chewing the scenery here."

00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:56.901
-"Outta here, outta here," yeah.
-"Give me my moment."

00:06:56.901 --> 00:06:58.234
-You don't want to do a dramatic

00:06:58.234 --> 00:06:59.100
scene with a monkey.
-"I'm going for an Oscar here."

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.501
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:00.501 --> 00:07:02.467
-This is her sixth nomination
for an Oscar for Crystal.

00:07:02.467 --> 00:07:04.334
She's so lucky.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.334 --> 00:07:08.200
But I think like I would be
a little afraid to like --

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.467
I feel like monkeys can like
feel your emotions or something,

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:13.601
so, if I got nervous,
I would make her nervous.

00:07:13.601 --> 00:07:15.934
and then maybe -- would she
attack me or something?

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:17.634
[ Laughter ]
-No. No. But they do --

00:07:17.634 --> 00:07:21.267
The monkey -- Her trainer said
that she does --

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:24.701
she likes to know -- she knows
instinctively who's in charge.

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:26.701
-Oh.
-And she befriends them.

00:07:26.701 --> 00:07:28.033
[ Laughter ]
-Wow! She can feel it.

00:07:28.033 --> 00:07:31.133
-So, she knew which one was
Steven Spielberg, yeah.

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:32.934
[ Laughter ]
-She's like --

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:34.834
Yeah, we got that take, right,
Steven? Alright.

00:07:34.834 --> 00:07:36.000
[ New York accent,
smoker's voice ]
Thank you. Perfect.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.200
[ Laughter ]
I appreciate it. Thanks a lot.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.234
Ready for Take 2?
I usually don't do two takes.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.067
Alright, here we go.

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:43.634
Let's talk about your new movie,
"Showing Up."

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:45.567
Congrats. You're getting
great reviews on this.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:49.801
It's a comedy.
How do we set up this film?

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:51.234
-So, this is the fourth movie
that I've made

00:07:51.234 --> 00:07:53.434
with my friend Kelly Reichardt.
-Yeah, you guys are

00:07:53.434 --> 00:07:55.133
great together.
-Yeah, we've been working
together for 15 years.

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:57.067
The first movie that we made,
"Wendy and Lucy,"

00:07:57.067 --> 00:07:58.968
was a crew of 13

00:07:58.968 --> 00:08:02.701
and so this, for us,
is a much bigger movie.

00:08:02.701 --> 00:08:05.300
-Wow! Thirteen people
on the whole set?

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.067
-Yeah, 13 people
on the whole set made a movie.

00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:08.567
-Isn't that great, though,
to do that?

00:08:08.567 --> 00:08:10.667
-That that's possible, yeah.
-That's possible
to make a movie.

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.133
"We're making a movie!"
-Yeah, it really is.

00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:14.334
So, I think like that's always
the core of the work that we do.

00:08:14.334 --> 00:08:16.934
You know, we've sort of --
we've trusted each other.

00:08:16.934 --> 00:08:19.067
We've respected each other
for such a long time

00:08:19.067 --> 00:08:22.767
and this movie --
actually, Judd Hirsch,

00:08:22.767 --> 00:08:25.767
Hong Chau, and myself --
I know, I know. So, she's --

00:08:25.767 --> 00:08:28.400
You know, three recent
Oscar nominees in this movie.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.834
-That's right.
"The Whale" and --

00:08:30.834 --> 00:08:32.467
Judd Hirsch -- "Fabelmans."
-Yeah.

00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:36.334
-Oh, my gosh! Can we set up --
What is the film about?

00:08:36.334 --> 00:08:38.400
-So, it's really about --
it's about being an artist

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.100
and it's about everything
that has to happen

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.267
before you can just sit down
at your table and make work.

00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:45.934
And are they things
that we do to ourselves

00:08:45.934 --> 00:08:47.234
or is it really
just circumstances

00:08:47.234 --> 00:08:48.501
that are beyond our control?

00:08:48.501 --> 00:08:51.000
And actually, are they part
of the creative process?

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.701
It's about how life gets
in the way

00:08:53.701 --> 00:08:55.434
or maybe is a part
of art making.

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:57.167
-Mm! [ Claps ] There we go.
I want to show a clip.

00:08:57.167 --> 00:08:58.901
Here's Michelle Williams
in "Showing up."

00:08:58.901 --> 00:09:00.567
Take a look at this.

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:01.968
-Would you give this
to your brother

00:09:01.968 --> 00:09:02.767
when you see him again?

00:09:07.467 --> 00:09:10.334
For five years,
he lived in that jacket.

00:09:10.334 --> 00:09:14.000
I mean, it was mine, but he
took possession. [ Laughs ]

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.000
He might like it again.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.701
-Yeah, I remember this.
-Yeah.

00:09:17.701 --> 00:09:19.367
-Have you talked to Sean?

00:09:19.367 --> 00:09:20.934
-Yeah, I talk to him
all the time.

00:09:20.934 --> 00:09:23.267
Come on, let's go.
-When's the last time
you talked to him?

00:09:23.267 --> 00:09:26.834
-He bought a delicious stuffing
for Thanksgiving.

00:09:26.834 --> 00:09:28.300
It was ravishing.
Let's go.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.467
-Dad.

00:09:29.467 --> 00:09:31.701
Dad, that was six months ago.

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:33.667
-Well, he was great
six months ago

00:09:33.667 --> 00:09:37.334
and his stuffing was superb.

00:09:37.334 --> 00:09:39.534
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Michelle Williams, everybody.

00:09:39.534 --> 00:09:42.367
"Showing Up" is
in theaters Friday.

00:09:42.367 --> 00:09:44.000
We'll be right back
with John Stamos.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:44.968
Stick around, everybody.

